## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!externalToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | `CancellationToken` is cooperative — if `work()` never checks the token, it keeps running in the background even after timeout. Pass a `CancellationToken` into `work` for true cancellation. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always use `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary to convert to `TimeoutException`. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If `work` calls a blocking API that can't accept a token (e.g., legacy library), it will continue running. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()`. |

### For Work That Cooperates with Cancellation

If you control the work delegate, accept a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                   .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                   .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Then the work checks the token:
```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` (automatic via `CancelAfter`) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Not needed — check `token.IsCancellationRequested` to decide whether to continue |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join(timeout)` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync(token)` |
